A. Schedule constants (Lemma 4.4, Theorem 4.7(d), 'Why the order S, U, S, H')
==============================================================================
S,U,S,H: scan's j-th step is query 2j-1: True; U's k-th draw is 4k-2: True; H's i-th entry is 4i: True
S,U,S,H: in every prefix, non-scan queries <= scan queries: True
SUSH: query index of the scan's n0-th step (worst case, nonempty list): n0=8: 15 (2n0-1=15), n0=9: 17 (2n0-1=17), n0=32: 63 (2n0-1=63), n0=33: 65 (2n0-1=65)
UHSS: query index of the scan's n0-th step (worst case, nonempty list): n0=8: 16 (2n0-1=15), n0=9: 19 (2n0-1=17), n0=32: 64 (2n0-1=63), n0=33: 67 (2n0-1=65)
HUSS: query index of the scan's n0-th step (worst case, nonempty list): n0=8: 16 (2n0-1=15), n0=9: 19 (2n0-1=17), n0=32: 64 (2n0-1=63), n0=33: 67 (2n0-1=65)
SUSH with an empty list: scan's 8th step at query 11
UHSS with an empty list: scan's 8th step at query 11

==============================================================================
B. Lemma 4.4: E[queries] <= min{4n/|M|, 2 j_S + 1, 4(j_H + 1)} (simulation)
==============================================================================
400 random rejected runs, 300 searches each: largest mean/bound = 1.000; searches above the sure bound min(2j_S+1, 4(j_H+1)): 0

==============================================================================
C. Proposition 4.10 (safety net for any learner): per-problem task queries, cache-first decoding
==============================================================================
n0=  4: search only (no L, no bizarre check, beta=0), 600 problems, 4 adversarial learners: max task queries per problem = 7 (2n0 = 8); outcomes {'output': 600}
n0=  8: search only (no L, no bizarre check, beta=0), 600 problems, 4 adversarial learners: max task queries per problem = 15 (2n0 = 16); outcomes {'output': 600}
n0= 16: search only (no L, no bizarre check, beta=0), 600 problems, 4 adversarial learners: max task queries per problem = 27 (2n0 = 32); outcomes {'output': 600}
n0=  4: with audits at beta=1: max task queries per problem = 8 (2n0 = 8). Searches alone never exceed 2n0-1 (a search ending in a scan hit has one fewer non-scan query, and c_{n0-1} can be scanned only as a mistake), so one audit still fits within 2n0.
n0=  8: with audits at beta=1: max task queries per problem = 15 (2n0 = 16). Searches alone never exceed 2n0-1 (a search ending in a scan hit has one fewer non-scan query, and c_{n0-1} can be scanned only as a mistake), so one audit still fits within 2n0.
perfect learner (M = 0), beta = 1, 50 problems: 0 rejections, 50 task queries (all audits); (c)'s 'at most 2 n0 M task queries in all' omits the audits.
n0= 16: if a cached scan step uses up the S turn (no query, but the turn passes to U), max task queries per problem = 204 (2n0 = 32): the proof needs the scan to skip cached notebooks within its turn.
n0=  8: with route (i) (L flags 'BAD'; (L0)-(L1) hold), a learner that writes a fresh wrong valid entry at c_0 and 'BAD' at step 1: 200 task queries, 200 rejections in 200 reruns, outcome 'cap' (2n0 = 16). c_0 is never queried, so it is never cached.
n0=  8: with the 'nothing bizarre' check ((M1)-(M2) hold), a learner that writes a fresh wrong entry at c_0, 'WEIRD' at step 1 and SOL at step 2: 200 task queries, 200 failed checks in 200 reruns, outcome 'cap'.
So (b) holds for route (ii) searches, with or without audits, when the scan skips cached notebooks within
its turn; with route (i) or the bizarre check it fails for an arbitrary learner.
(c)'s count of rejections still holds in all cases (each rejection or failed check ends at a told notebook
where the learner was wrong); its total of task queries needs the audits added.

==============================================================================
D. Theorem 6.2(b): B_T against log2 C(T+K', K')
==============================================================================
thread V Theorem 2(b): against VSI[coin_q], q = K'/(K'+T), the exponent is B_coin = T log2(1+K'/T) + K' log2(1+T/K');
against the uniform mixture of content-blind verdict scripts it is exactly log2 C(T+K', K').
  K_      T     log2 C     B_coin    Klog2e+Klog2(1+T/K)  B_coin <= log2 C ?
   1      4       2.32       3.61                   3.76  False
   4      6       7.71       9.71                  11.06  False
  10     10      17.50      20.00                  24.43  False
  10    100      45.41      48.34                  49.02  False
  20   1000     138.54     142.02                 142.30  False
  50  10000     450.36     454.51                 454.69  False
So 'B_T <= log2 C(T+K',K')' (paper) is true only for the script-mixture reference; for the coin reference B_T exceeds log2 C. Both are below K' log2 e + K' log2(1+T/K').

